#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "298d3ee7-bb43-5835-8dc5-3306d1f9841f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Special Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4243ff74-259e-5230-becf-285644e45f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4243ff74-259e-5230-becf-285644e45f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250147} :answer-field/id #uuid "814efb60-5471-5ee7-a6c4-d64b4126f57d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93039f81-d254-59b7-bb23-afc67d93c39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167566" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842e4736-6732-5d54-8ee1-7b1f768523d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "842e4736-6732-5d54-8ee1-7b1f768523d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250150} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac2db53-238c-5be1-8039-5b40526f1777" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250149}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61ff54f9-7eef-527f-ad64-ca38683902ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-167567" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3e27fd-fe2a-5723-821f-acf7a4f9ddaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\csc(t)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3e27fd-fe2a-5723-821f-acf7a4f9ddaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\csc(t)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250153} :answer-field/id #uuid "50569ae4-39a3-5913-8892-6604eeae4381" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60eda413-97e0-5606-bb17-3d5a70d41bae" :question/math-academy-id "q-167568" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=4u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{csc}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4{csc}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9836abbe-e172-5c2f-8b19-145ef6d5c02d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218a4b91-c70a-5d7a-bd0f-36310f6f2df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "218a4b91-c70a-5d7a-bd0f-36310f6f2df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cot(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250156} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b03522-97ff-5129-abe9-98947e7a422f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3047aa7e-6cb6-5d26-9ed3-c77c14702d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167569" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\csc(t)^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c5a9d62-eab8-5f09-aeb7-7a9a1c4e6eb1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio of a Larger Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538265ee-1061-5103-982e-a2941124ad24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "538265ee-1061-5103-982e-a2941124ad24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250159} :answer-field/id #uuid "329508af-47f0-5285-ba36-769738acc228" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c74073fa-e61f-5b08-b5be-fb67408d3773" :question/math-academy-id "q-167570" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\csc(t)^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cot(t)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nAnother particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})=(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9368b4e-a855-5fab-b074-a3a505c7ab0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88045b8c-fff1-5b24-b4a0-99e9010d3d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88045b8c-fff1-5b24-b4a0-99e9010d3d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250162} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abfe584-47b5-5880-be5a-1102712a9086" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250161}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a63a20d7-c72a-5eae-a8cb-367b1d6eb36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167571" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\csc(t)^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cot(t)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nAnother particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc481c1f-37f1-51f1-aa3d-6c60e24ea9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{t}{2}\\sin(\\frac{t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc481c1f-37f1-51f1-aa3d-6c60e24ea9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{t}{2}\\sin(\\frac{t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250165} :answer-field/id #uuid "289e929a-42b7-5bc0-8b38-26ba16103189" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d736a850-66a6-5820-ac9d-0fa81a1d204a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167572" :question/problem "The velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, of a subatomic particle is modeled using the function $v(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t)$ for $\\pi <t<2\\pi .$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\csc(t)^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cot(t)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\text{ }C$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nAnother particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}(\\frac{16\\pi }{3})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nFind ${lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{5\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))+(t)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
